<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Schumann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1810">1810</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Schumann was a German composer, pianist and music critic of the early Romantic era, born in Zwickau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, who composed in all the main musical genres of his time. He studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and piano with Friedrich Wieck from 1829, but a worsening problem with his right hand turned him to composition, and in 1834 he co-founded the Neue Zeitschrift für Musik, which he edited for ten years. In 1840 he married Wieck's daughter Clara against her father's bitter opposition, then wrote song cycles such as Dichterliebe and, in 1841, the first of his four symphonies. In 1854 he threw himself into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and died two years later in a sanatorium near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-schumann.jpg" alt="Robert Schumann" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Kriehuber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Schumann_1839.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Schumann was born on 8 June 1810 in Zwickau, in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, the fifth and last child of August Schumann and his wife Johanna Christiane. His father was a bookseller, lexicographer, author and publisher who made considerable sums from German translations of Cervantes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>, and Robert, his favourite child, spent many hours exploring the classics in his father's collection. August died in 1826, and Schumann's mother, less enthusiastic about a musical career, persuaded him to study law. He entered Leipzig University in 1828, but reading and playing the piano occupied much of his time. He discovered the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-schubert">Franz Schubert</a>, whose death in November 1828 made him cry all night.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Schumann published his Op. 1, piano variations on a theme spelled from the name of Countess Pauline von Abegg, who was almost certainly his own invention. The use of a musical cryptogram became a recurrent feature of his later music. In 1832 he published Papillons, a programmatic piano piece. He came to regard himself as having two sides to his personality and art, and named his introspective self Eusebius and his impetuous alter ego Florestan. In 1840 he turned to song, producing more than half his total output of Lieder, including Myrthen, a wedding present for Clara, Frauenliebe und Leben and Dichterliebe.</p><p>In 1841 he focused on orchestral music. His First Symphony, The Spring, was premiered by Mendelssohn at the Gewandhaus on 31 March, at a concert where Clara also played. In 1842 he wrote three string quartets, a Piano Quintet, written for Clara, and a Piano Quartet. His opera Genoveva, premiered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in June 1850, was not the success he hoped for, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> revived it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1855, the only other production in Schumann's lifetime. Schumann wrote more than 300 songs for voice and piano, favouring such later Romantic poets as Heine, Eichendorff and Mörike.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Schumann moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> in 1850 as the city's director of music but resigned after three years, since his shyness and mental instability made it hard to work with his orchestra. In 1853 he met the twenty-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a> and praised him in the Neue Zeitschrift für Musik. The following year his mental health deteriorated gravely. He threw himself into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, was rescued and was taken to a private sanatorium near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, where he died on 29 July 1856.</p><p>His reputation rests chiefly on the piano works and songs of the 1830s and 1840s. Later composers drew on him, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Mahler</a>, Richard Strauss, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a>, Bizet, Fauré, Debussy and Ravel. Elgar called him his ideal, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a>, though critical of his orchestration, described him as a composer of genius.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Schumann” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Austria, when <b>Robert Schumann</b> quoted it in 1839, singing it was forbidden as a piece that might cause public disorder.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseph von Eichendorff also used the figure in his poem &quot;Waldesgespräch&quot; (1812), later set by <b>Robert Schumann</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexandrov described the music as a march joined to Russian traditional music, above all the bylina epic songs, and it shares chord progressions with Vasily Kalinnikov's overture Bylina and has been compared with <b>Robert Schumann</b>'s &quot;Frühlingsfahrt&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/state-anthem-of-the-soviet-union">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zwickau, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Endenich, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.72,12.5&amp;year=1839" aria-label="Zwickau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4835 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Schumann"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5285 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5285 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.72,12.5&amp;year=1839">What was here in 1839? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/clara-schumann"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/clara-schumann.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Clara Schumann</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 1819–1896</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Play strictly in time! The playing of many a virtuoso resembles the walk of an intoxicated person.&#8221;</p><footer>Advice to Young Musicians (1850) &#183; tr. Henry Hugo Pierson, 1860 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28219/pg28219-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Endeavour to play easy pieces well and with elegance; that is better than to play difficult pieces badly.&#8221;</p><footer>Advice to Young Musicians (1850) &#183; tr. Henry Hugo Pierson, 1860 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28219/pg28219-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The object of art is not to produce riches. Become a great artist, and all other desirable accessories will fall to your lot.&#8221;</p><footer>Advice to Young Musicians (1850) &#183; tr. Henry Hugo Pierson, 1860 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28219/pg28219-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
